Wall Street had a big day on news of a  possible trade agreement between the US  and China the Dow gained 298 today the  S&amp;P 500 was up 20 the Nasdaq finished  ahead 40 President Trump is celebrating  this development but some experts are  saying China may be pulling a fast one  on the US and there is already a  significant amount of hedging from both  sides tonight  correspondent Kristen Fisher reports  from the White House to see before  presiding over the swearing-in ceremony  of the new CIA director Gina Haspel  president Trump unloaded on Twitter  about his administration's approach to  resolving a trade war with China noting  that China has agreed to buy massive  amounts of additional farm and  agricultural products and that barriers  and tariffs will come down after months  of threatening to impose stiff tariffs  on Chinese steel and aluminum the  Treasury Secretary made this bold  declaration on Fox News Sunday we're  putting the trade we're on on hold so  right now we are we have agreed to put  the tariffs on hold while we try to  execute the framework secretary minuchin  says that framework includes China's  agreement to substantially reduce the  trade deficit by increasing their  purchases of goods but when asked  exactly by how much the secretary  wouldn't say there was a reason why we  didn't put a specific number and I would  also say this is a specific number again  whatever we agree to these are not  government to government purchases we  have very specific targets industry by  industry these now  the Trump administration describes last  week's talks with the Chinese and the  framework it produced has substantial  progress and the markets responded  accordingly his fears of a global trade  war between the world's two biggest  economies subsided but today there are  bipartisan fears that this potential  deal is letting the Chinese off the hook  on one of its biggest transgressions  Democratic Senator Chuck Schumer urged  the Trump administration to not sell out  for a temporary purchase of goods  without addressing the real issue the  theft of American intellectual property  while Republican Senator Marco Rubio  said any deal that doesn't stop China  from cyber theft and forcing US  companies to transfer technology to them  is a huge loss so it's unclear if that  issue the theft of American intellectual  property is still on the negotiating  table as for what's next the Commerce  Secretary Wilbur Ross is heading to  China next week to try to turn this  initial framework into an actual signed  agreement Brett Kristen Fisher live at  the White House Kristen thank you
 

